Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger have initiated a year-long process of withdrawing from the International Criminal Court, a move human rights defenders say will leave victims of war crimes without access to justice.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/940e9aaa-988d-11f0-9a73-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-09-23T073433Z-1284941231-RC20XGAHRUAC-RTRMADP-3-SAHEL-JUSTICE-ICCT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/940e9aaa-988d-11f0-9a73-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-09-23T073433Z-1284941231-RC20XGAHRUAC-RTRMADP-3-SAHEL-JUSTICE-ICCT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6cdb6a62-76b8-11f1-8eae-a3670709bd60</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 13:11:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/940e9aaa-988d-11f0-9a73-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2025-09-23T073433Z-1284941231-RC20XGAHRUAC-RTRMADP-3-SAHEL-JUSTICE-ICCT.jpg">REUTERS - Piroschka Van De Wouw</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DRC trials experimental treatment for rare Ebola strain as outbreak worsens</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260703-drc-trials-experimental-treatment-for-rare-ebola-strain-as-outbreak-worsens</link><description>
The Democratic Republic of Congo has launched a clinical trial of experimental Ebola treatments as the outbreak continues to spread in the north-east of the country. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b29ced5c-7661-11f1-9665-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-02T204519Z-2059851712-RC2R5MAKUXM2-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-CONGO-SOUTHAFRICA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b29ced5c-7661-11f1-9665-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-02T204519Z-2059851712-RC2R5MAKUXM2-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-CONGO-SOUTHAFRICA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">12d998d6-76b8-11f1-bc7c-09e15d4a7f4e</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 09:51:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b29ced5c-7661-11f1-9665-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-07-02T204519Z-2059851712-RC2R5MAKUXM2-RTRMADP-3-HEALTH-EBOLA-CONGO-SOUTHAFRICA.jpg">© Arsene Mpiana / REUTERS</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Belgium opens up Congo archives amid global minerals race</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260703-belgium-opens-up-congo-archives-amid-global-minerals-race</link><description>
From century-old maps to meticulous field notes, Belgium is sitting on a trove of geological records on colonial-era Congo – a coveted archive it is working to open up amid a global scramble for critical minerals.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf8cef44-769d-11f1-ba2c-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/df97c1047b59f80f9d9e67545125c77d2a6144c7.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf8cef44-769d-11f1-ba2c-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/df97c1047b59f80f9d9e67545125c77d2a6144c7.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c06c3dac-769d-11f1-aff4-c152c92ae64f</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2026 05:12:12 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf8cef44-769d-11f1-ba2c-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/df97c1047b59f80f9d9e67545125c77d2a6144c7.jpg">© ERIC LALMAND / Belga/AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>US shows ‘increasingly blatant’ bias over DRC peace deal, says Rwandan minister</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260702-us-shows-increasingly-blatant-bias-over-drc-peace-deal-says-rwandan-fm-olivier-nduhungirehe</link><description>
The fragile peace process between Rwanda and the Democratic Republic of Congo is under renewed pressure, as the United States imposes fresh sanctions over alleged links to mineral trafficking from areas controlled by M23 rebels. Rwandan Foreign Minister Olivier Nduhungirehe tells RFI Kigali is being unfairly blamed – arguing that the Congolese government has failed to meet its own commitments under the US-brokered peace deal.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fb834410-4076-11f1-9d89-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-33CY3LW.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fb834410-4076-11f1-9d89-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-33CY3LW.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d8f5ef0c-7617-11f1-bd46-ef6f72536f63</guid><pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 15:58:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fb834410-4076-11f1-9d89-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-33CY3LW.jpg">© Alexis Huguet / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Algeria votes in parliamentary elections amid public apathy and football fever</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260702-algeria-votes-in-parliamentary-elections-amid-public-apathy-and-football-fever</link><description>
Algerians are voting in parliamentary elections on Thursday, with cost of living concerns, questions over political freedoms and the national football team’s World Cup run all competing for public attention.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/87002b0c-75e2-11f1-a0e7-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/algeria2bis.jpeg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/87002b0c-75e2-11f1-a0e7-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/algeria2bis.jpeg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">23d54ece-75e0-11f1-b2c6-e7d878263f35</guid><pubDate>Thu, 02 Jul 2026 07:12:43 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/87002b0c-75e2-11f1-a0e7-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/algeria2bis.jpeg">© Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Mali's war on civilians deepens as alliances shift, HRW says</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260701-human-rights-watch-reports-grave-abuses-from-all-sides-in-mali-since-april-attacks</link><description>
Human Rights Watch (HRW) says the army, allied militias and Islamist armed groups have all committed abuses with impunity since the attacks in northern Mali in April.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a91e28b0-4243-11f1-b76f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A92J2CE-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a91e28b0-4243-11f1-b76f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A92J2CE-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e2bd846e-73a6-11f1-8bc3-cb75072d777a</guid><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 12:58:36 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/a91e28b0-4243-11f1-b76f-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-A92J2CE-1.jpg">AFP - ABDOLLAH AG MOHAMED</source><dc:creator>Melissa Chemam</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Sudan's RSF paramilitaries accused of crimes against humanity in El Fasher</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260701-sudan-rsf-accused-of-crimes-against-humanity-in-el-fasher-amnesty</link><description>
Sudan's paramilitary Rapid Support Forces committed crimes against humanity and ethnic cleansing while deliberately targeting children during their campaign to seize El Fasher, the capital of North Darfur state, Amnesty International said on Wednesday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/cebe6a40-7532-11f1-b380-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25341684040183.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/cebe6a40-7532-11f1-b380-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25341684040183.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e249049a-7531-11f1-b895-ad9b86483aa0</guid><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 10:29:56 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/cebe6a40-7532-11f1-b380-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25341684040183.jpg">AP - Marwan Ali</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>South Africa protesters confront migrant homes during nationwide marches</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260701-south-africa-protesters-confront-migrant-homes-during-nationwide-marches</link><description>
Protesters stopped outside buildings believed to house undocumented migrants and urged residents to leave as thousands marched across South Africa on Tuesday, the deadline set by anti-immigration groups for foreigners without papers to leave the country.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c8d8d5f6-74b1-11f1-992f-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-30T165841Z-969527858-RC2C4MA8UPF8-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c8d8d5f6-74b1-11f1-992f-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-30T165841Z-969527858-RC2C4MA8UPF8-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">461c0fbe-7526-11f1-8b2d-7129b96364f7</guid><pubDate>Wed, 01 Jul 2026 08:56:38 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/c8d8d5f6-74b1-11f1-992f-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-30T165841Z-969527858-RC2C4MA8UPF8-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg">© Rogan Ward / Reuters</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>South Africa braces for violence as anti-migrant marches reach boiling point</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260630-south-africa-braces-for-voilence-as-anti-migrant-marches-reach-boiling-point</link><description>
South African police were deployed in force across the country on Tuesday to forestall unrest and protests, as anti-immigrant groups' unofficial deadline for undocumented foreign nationals to leave the country fell. It follows a weeks-long campaign that has sent thousands fleeing and claimed four lives.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8fa55aec-7421-11f1-8dae-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-26T125459Z-224291328-RC231MADV813-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8fa55aec-7421-11f1-8dae-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-26T125459Z-224291328-RC231MADV813-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ce23e162-7471-11f1-9b25-432894e2c7a6</guid><pubDate>Tue, 30 Jun 2026 14:20:56 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8fa55aec-7421-11f1-8dae-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-26T125459Z-224291328-RC231MADV813-RTRMADP-3-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg">REUTERS - Siyabonga Sishi</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Uganda dissidents on trial as president’s son tightens control</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260630-uganda-dissidents-on-trial-as-president-s-son-increases-his-grip-on-freedoms</link><description>
Uganda's army chief, General Kainerugaba, has ordered the arrest of leading activists and politicians in recent weeks, and this weekend shut down the country's main independent media group. Two of those arrested appear in court this Tuesday: opposition figure Kizza Besigye and his lawyer.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aade6dd0-6a95-11f0-8251-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-47VP4CX.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aade6dd0-6a95-11f0-8251-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-47VP4CX.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">12325f44-745b-11f1-8210-c7775ee2d415</guid><pubDate>Tue, 30 Jun 2026 11:18:16 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/aade6dd0-6a95-11f0-8251-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-47VP4CX.jpg">© Badru Katumba / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Uganda's army chief orders shutdown of country's biggest media group</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260629-uganda-s-army-chief-orders-shutdown-of-country-s-biggest-media-group</link><description>
The most prominent media outlets in Uganda remain unable to operate after the army chief, Muhoozi Kainerugaba, the son of President Museveni and his likely successor, ordered the military shutdown on Sunday of the country's largest media group's headquarters in Kampala. The move has drawn widespread criticism.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8cf07826-1001-11ea-bd2d-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/2013-05-20T180850Z_1980301761_GM1E95L05EU01_RTRMADP_2_UGANDA-POLITICS.JPG"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8cf07826-1001-11ea-bd2d-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/2013-05-20T180850Z_1980301761_GM1E95L05EU01_RTRMADP_2_UGANDA-POLITICS.JPG" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c3c525c0-739d-11f1-979b-4525f3b88988</guid><pubDate>Mon, 29 Jun 2026 15:54:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8cf07826-1001-11ea-bd2d-005056bf7c53/w:1024/p:16x9/2013-05-20T180850Z_1980301761_GM1E95L05EU01_RTRMADP_2_UGANDA-POLITICS.JPG">Reuters/James Akena</source><dc:creator>Melissa Chemam</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Uganda army chief shuts down media group amid widespread curb on free speech</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260628-uganda-army-chief-shuts-down-media-group-amid-widespread-curb-on-free-speech</link><description>
Uganda's leading independent media group said it was under "military siege" on Sunday after the army chief ordered the shutdown of its newspapers, TV station and radio outlets. This comes amid a widespread crackdown on free speech, initiated by President Yoweri Museveni's son.   
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d8e138a2-72d2-11f1-b9bb-31dfa77f3cba</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 09:34:59 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/198e8cec-6987-11f1-ba6f-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/000-B2Q638G.jpg">AFP - BADRU KATUMBA</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>'A wake up call': Municipal misery hangs over upcoming South African elections</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260628-municipal-misery-weighs-on-looming-south-african-elections-november-anc-da-black-townships</link><description>
Mounds of garbage, potholed roads and sewage spills: grim conditions like these led voters near Johannesburg to abandon their long-time loyalty to the African National Congress and hand the rival Democratic Alliance its first black township ward in South Africa, highlighting frustration over municipal-level failures ahead of November's local elections.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fbee75cc-7056-11f1-a2a9-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/d65c677c6631d46e2ac4b0feba0a4e5cff507d20.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fbee75cc-7056-11f1-a2a9-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/d65c677c6631d46e2ac4b0feba0a4e5cff507d20.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">10cc8f86-7158-11f1-87dc-2b2bc81ca91c</guid><pubDate>Sun, 28 Jun 2026 08:57:50 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/fbee75cc-7056-11f1-a2a9-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/d65c677c6631d46e2ac4b0feba0a4e5cff507d20.jpg">© PHILL MAGAKOE / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Burkina Faso junta cuts diplomatic ties with former colonial ruler France</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260627-burkina-faso-junta-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-former-colonial-ruler-france</link><description>
Burkina Faso's ruling junta, lead by Captain Ibrahim Traoré, announced late Friday it has cut diplomatic ties with former colonial ruler France, accusing Paris of persistently acting against its interests. France called the decision "hostile and baseless".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b2e6679a-7191-11f1-93d9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/facebook-rtb-annonce-rutpure-relations-diplomatiques-avec-la-france.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b2e6679a-7191-11f1-93d9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/facebook-rtb-annonce-rutpure-relations-diplomatiques-avec-la-france.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8992fe3c-71fd-11f1-8f9a-7337819a1f80</guid><pubDate>Sat, 27 Jun 2026 07:59:41 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/b2e6679a-7191-11f1-93d9-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/facebook-rtb-annonce-rutpure-relations-diplomatiques-avec-la-france.jpg">© RTB (screen grab)</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>South African anti-migrant groups defend planned ‘Liberation March’</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260625-south-african-anti-migrant-groups-defend-planned-liberation-march</link><description>
A coalition of South African organisations campaigning against undocumented migrants has defended its plans for nationwide demonstrations on 30 June, insisting the marches will be peaceful despite rising tensions and recent deadly unrest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ca5ddffc-5576-11f1-8e57-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-13T100315Z-487464677-RC28YKAW2Q98-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ca5ddffc-5576-11f1-8e57-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-13T100315Z-487464677-RC28YKAW2Q98-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">733e3b86-7079-11f1-b2f0-d975a6e15264</guid><pubDate>Thu, 25 Jun 2026 13:13:53 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/ca5ddffc-5576-11f1-8e57-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-05-13T100315Z-487464677-RC28YKAW2Q98-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SAFRICA-XENOPHOBIA.jpg">© Reuters/Ihsaan Haffejee</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Kenya mounts huge security operation on Gen Z protest anniversary</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260625-kenya-police-in-massive-show-of-force-on-protest-anniversary</link><description>
Kenyan police put on a massive show of force Thursday in a bid to prevent protests marking two years since a landmark Gen Z uprising.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/593ee4f0-706b-11f1-99cd-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/6ff654a80a9517e713700f1a9012fbfb067f7cb1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/593ee4f0-706b-11f1-99cd-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/6ff654a80a9517e713700f1a9012fbfb067f7cb1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a250730-705a-11f1-8bbc-f96cf60cd9d0</guid><pubDate>Thu, 25 Jun 2026 05:54:11 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/593ee4f0-706b-11f1-99cd-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/6ff654a80a9517e713700f1a9012fbfb067f7cb1.jpg">© Luis TATO / AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Latest Niamey airport attack tests Niger junta as terror groups 'adapt'</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260624-latest-niamey-airport-attack-tests-niger-junta-as-terror-groups-adapt</link><description>
Niamey airport was attacked for the second time this year on 18 June, with 13 people killed, in an assault that illustrated how jihadist groups are changing their methods to test Niger’s security forces. Nigerien researcher Brimaka Abdoul Azizou Garba tells RFI the attack showed terrorism is “not abating” but adapting.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/2159fc1a-6c5a-11f1-b030-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-19T041459Z-311690609-RC2RWLA8S6JE-RTRMADP-3-NIGER-SECURITY.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/2159fc1a-6c5a-11f1-b030-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-19T041459Z-311690609-RC2RWLA8S6JE-RTRMADP-3-NIGER-SECURITY.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f0da3872-6e0f-11f1-925b-055a56631484</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 20:10:59 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/2159fc1a-6c5a-11f1-b030-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-19T041459Z-311690609-RC2RWLA8S6JE-RTRMADP-3-NIGER-SECURITY.jpg">via REUTERS - ORTN</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>US releases experimental Ebola drug for DR Congo outbreak trials</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260624-us-releases-experimental-ebola-drug-for-dr-congo-outbreak-trials</link><description>
Experimental Ebola drugs are being shipped to Democratic Republic of Congo as the United States releases doses of a treatment for clinical trials in a widening outbreak that has caused more than 1,000 cases and over 250 deaths, the World Health Organization and US officials have said.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/480f18f2-6e32-11f1-be90-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26169798701927.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/480f18f2-6e32-11f1-be90-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26169798701927.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3e9c618e-6faa-11f1-afd8-05facb5211a6</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 10:32:10 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/480f18f2-6e32-11f1-be90-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26169798701927.jpg">© AP/Moses Sawasawa</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Seven European countries urge ‘immediate halt’ to Sudan violence</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260624-seven-european-countries-urge-immediate-halt-to-sudan-violence-el-obeid</link><description>
Seven European countries have called on Sudan's paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) to immediately stop their assault on El-Obeid, a city in Sudan's North Kordofan state, warning of credible signs that a major offensive could be imminent as civilians face worsening shortages of food, water and fuel.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d29843ac-6f9c-11f1-acff-3b2add01b4bf</guid><pubDate>Wed, 24 Jun 2026 07:28:51 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/556b7ef0-374b-11f1-9147-005056bfb2b6/w:1024/p:16x9/000-824J2WE-1.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Kenya halts US Ebola facility, health minister apologises for disobeying orders</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260623-kenya-halts-us-ebola-facility-health-minister-apologises-for-disobeying-orders</link><description>
Kenya has ordered a halt to preparations for a controversial US-run Ebola quarantine facility, the health minister told a court Tuesday, after being held in contempt for ignoring a previous stop-work order. The project has prompted widespread anger, and several people were killed during protests earlier this month.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/653c33fe-6ede-11f1-9533-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/kenya-health-afp-june-2026-000-B6VG9XG.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/653c33fe-6ede-11f1-9533-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/kenya-health-afp-june-2026-000-B6VG9XG.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e03b02f8-6edc-11f1-84e1-a769c4b1a184</guid><pubDate>Tue, 23 Jun 2026 13:41:09 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/653c33fe-6ede-11f1-9533-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/kenya-health-afp-june-2026-000-B6VG9XG.jpg">AFP - SIMON MAINA</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight on Africa</category><title>Blood minerals and memory: the Great Lakes in focus</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/podcasts/spotlight-on-africa/20260623-blood-minerals-and-memory-the-great-lakes-in-focus</link><description>
This week, Spotlight on Africa turns to the Great Lakes region. First, a new Global Witness report reveals how coltan is being smuggled out of the Democratic Republic of Congo, amid the ongoing conflict in the east, through Rwanda and on to companies worldwide. Then, artist Grada Kilomba discusses her journey to create a unique monument for Paris commemorating the 1994 Rwandan genocide.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/4986594a-61ba-11f1-91d5-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/IMG-3755.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/4986594a-61ba-11f1-91d5-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/IMG-3755.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">08e27166-6990-11f1-937f-2d72166d7bb4</guid><pubDate>Tue, 23 Jun 2026 10:43:57 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Melissa Chemam</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>UN warns of an 'imminent risk of mass atrocities' in Sudan's Kordofan</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260622-un-warns-of-an-imminent-risk-of-mass-atrocities-in-sudan-s-kordofan</link><description>
In Sudan, NGOs and the UN have warned of an "imminent risk of mass atrocities" in El-Obeid, a major city in North Kordofan. This comes as the Rapid Support Forces (RSF) step up their campaign against the regular army in the region.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17310110-6e36-11f1-82c5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/SUDAN-SURVIVORS-rtr-june-2026.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17310110-6e36-11f1-82c5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/SUDAN-SURVIVORS-rtr-june-2026.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8af1b034-6e29-11f1-8924-bb6928ded532</guid><pubDate>Mon, 22 Jun 2026 14:32:16 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/17310110-6e36-11f1-82c5-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/SUDAN-SURVIVORS-rtr-june-2026.jpg">REUTERS - Amr Abdallah Dalsh</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>DRC announces free healthcare for all illnesses in Ituri as Ebola gains ground</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260622-drc-announces-free-healthcare-for-all-illnesses-in-ituri-as-ebola-gains-ground</link><description>
The government of the Democratic Republic of Congo has announced the introduction of free healthcare for all diseases in Ituri – a pilot project that authorities hope to extend nationwide. This comes as the country struggles to contain an Ebola outbreak, with confirmed cases now at over 1,000.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bb571dd6-6e1f-11f1-918b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Ebola-DRC-afp-2026-000-B7NT9AJ.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bb571dd6-6e1f-11f1-918b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Ebola-DRC-afp-2026-000-B7NT9AJ.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8aa60cce-6e1f-11f1-abc9-ddc4fdda7727</guid><pubDate>Mon, 22 Jun 2026 10:58:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bb571dd6-6e1f-11f1-918b-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/Ebola-DRC-afp-2026-000-B7NT9AJ.jpg">AFP - JOSPIN MWISHA</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ethiopia’s ruling party wins another large majority in parliament</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260622-ethiopia-s-ruling-party-wins-another-large-majority-in-parliament</link><description>
Ethiopia's ruling party has won over 90 percent of seats in parliament, according to official results announced three weeks after the election.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf246d7c-6dd1-11f1-b235-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26153239514833.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf246d7c-6dd1-11f1-b235-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26153239514833.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4301cfd4-6dfc-11f1-8807-f1d2c1a8159b</guid><pubDate>Mon, 22 Jun 2026 05:36:04 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/bf246d7c-6dd1-11f1-b235-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26153239514833.jpg">© Amanuel Sileshi/AP</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>'One injection protects up to six months': what is new HIV drug lenacapavir?</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260621-lenacapavir-the-new-hiv-treatment-one-injection-can-protect-up-to-six-months</link><description>
As South Africa becomes the ninth African country to introduce the use of new long-acting drug lenacapavir for HIV prevention, RFI spoke with infectious disease specialist Ndong Essomba Bitchoka about how the treatment works, whether the supply will meet demand and why developing an HIV vaccine remains a challenge.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0334cf44-6660-11f1-afb6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-86VN3UQ.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0334cf44-6660-11f1-afb6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-86VN3UQ.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d17b171e-663d-11f1-bbec-0f2f458bf2a4</guid><pubDate>Sun, 21 Jun 2026 15:00:48 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/0334cf44-6660-11f1-afb6-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/000-86VN3UQ.jpg">© AFP - IHSAAN HAFFEJEE </source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Middle East war drives up bitumen costs for African road builders</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260621-middle-east-war-drives-up-bitumen-costs-for-african-road-builders</link><description>
War in the Middle East is pushing up the price of bitumen, the material used to make asphalt and road surfaces – and the effects are being felt across Africa, where few countries produce their own. Higher costs and longer delivery times are forcing firms from Madagascar to Guinea to Cameroon to renegotiate contracts, absorb rising expenses and rethink how they manage supplies.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60a12492-7cb7-11ee-bec0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/GettyImages-1181457172.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60a12492-7cb7-11ee-bec0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/GettyImages-1181457172.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1016ef86-6a42-11f1-b296-335bf50e7fa3</guid><pubDate>Sun, 21 Jun 2026 09:15:21 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60a12492-7cb7-11ee-bec0-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/GettyImages-1181457172.jpg">© Getty Images / Photo and Co</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Global partnership brings hope amid high pedestrian road deaths in Nairobi</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260621-global-partnership-brings-hope-amid-high-pedestrian-road-deaths-in-nairobi</link><description>
With pedestrians accounting for up to half of road deaths in Kenya, experts say the rapid expansion of the country's capital Nairobi's road network has failed to take into account how many journeys still begin on foot.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3d0ebf7a-6b26-11f1-a4cd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-Par8259750.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3d0ebf7a-6b26-11f1-a4cd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-Par8259750.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1052a566-63f9-11f1-b1a8-092b5aafc827</guid><pubDate>Sun, 21 Jun 2026 07:03:52 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/3d0ebf7a-6b26-11f1-a4cd-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/000-Par8259750.jpg">© AFP - TONY KARUMBA </source><dc:creator>Anne Macharia in Nairobi</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>'United front' on slavery reparations after historic summit in Ghana</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260620-united-front-on-slavery-reparations-after-historic-summit-in-ghana</link><description>
Countries pushing for reparations for slavery have hailed a milestone after adopting a shared manifesto for reparatory justice at a global summit in Ghana. Participants said the conference had given new momentum to their fight on both sides of the Atlantic.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/877c2bf2-6c29-11f1-ab07-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26170726373073.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/877c2bf2-6c29-11f1-ab07-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26170726373073.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8f8363ba-6c83-11f1-9f98-f367e4bc7a95</guid><pubDate>Sat, 20 Jun 2026 10:19:20 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/877c2bf2-6c29-11f1-ab07-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26170726373073.jpg">© AP Photo/Reuben Ekow Quansah </source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Sexual violence surges in Mozambique's Cabo Delgado as war grinds on</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260619-sexual-violence-surges-in-mozambique-s-cabo-delgado-as-war-grinds-on-forbidden-stories</link><description>
Sexual violence is surging in Mozambique's Cabo Delgado province, where women and girls are trapped between insurgents and soldiers after nearly a decade of conflict. Rich in natural gas, precious stones and lithium, the northern province has been scarred by attacks from Islamist militants and reprisals by Mozambique's armed forces. This seventh instalment of Mozambique Exposed – an investigation coordinated by Forbidden Stories to which RFI contributed – examines how women have paid the price.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60008716-696e-11f1-a710-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Cabo-Delgado-violences-sexuelles.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60008716-696e-11f1-a710-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Cabo-Delgado-violences-sexuelles.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">de1b3912-6bf2-11f1-a7e5-3d25ec2b6e04</guid><pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 16:08:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/60008716-696e-11f1-a710-005056bf30b7/w:1024/p:16x9/Cabo-Delgado-violences-sexuelles.png">© Studio FMM</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Ghana summit charts path from 'recognition to action' on slavery reparations</title><link>https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20260619-ghana-summit-charts-path-from-recognition-to-action-on-slavery-reparations</link><description>
Ghana is seeking to turn growing international support for slavery reparations into concrete action. As a three-day summit in the country concludes on Friday, African and Caribbean leaders are due to adopt a declaration setting out the next steps. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8f3c4b90-6b68-11f1-99e9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-18T221112Z-1993001739-RC2AWLAI1MJG-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SLAVERY.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8f3c4b90-6b68-11f1-99e9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-18T221112Z-1993001739-RC2AWLAI1MJG-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SLAVERY.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8eb05c08-6bee-11f1-bb86-07862faa65fd</guid><pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 15:54:37 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.rfi.fr/media/display/8f3c4b90-6b68-11f1-99e9-005056a97e36/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-06-18T221112Z-1993001739-RC2AWLAI1MJG-RTRMADP-3-GHANA-SLAVERY.jpg">REUTERS - Francis Kokoroko</source><dc:creator>RFI</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
